# Keep the build context to what the image actually compiles from.
#
# Two reasons, and the second is the important one:
#   * speed — build/ alone is gigabytes of object files the image rebuilds anyway
#   * hygiene — a site descriptor or a private key that wanders into the tree
#     must not be baked into an image that gets shared. The site conf is MOUNTED
#     at run time (see docker/Dockerfile), never copied in.
build/
build-*/
dut/env/orchestrator/target/

# Nested build trees too — a .dockerignore pattern without ** matches only the
# context root, and the one that bites is third_party/vsomeip/build: its
# CMakeCache.txt records the HOST's absolute source path, and cmake refuses a
# cache whose directory does not match. setup-vsomeip.sh cannot save us there —
# its pristine reset is `git clean -fdx -e build`, deliberately PRESERVING that
# directory so a developer keeps an incremental build. Correct on a workstation,
# poison in an image.
**/build/

# The site's npm tree is neither built nor served here.
site/node_modules/
*.pcap
*.log

# NOTE: .git/ is deliberately NOT excluded. scripts/setup-vsomeip.sh resets the
# vendored vsomeip tree with `git checkout -- .` + `git clean -fdx` before
# applying the quilt series, and third_party/vsomeip/.git is a 47-byte pointer
# into .git/modules/ — drop the parent and the submodule stops being a git
# checkout, which the script refuses (correctly) rather than building something
# unintended. It costs 64 MB of context; the alternative is an image built from
# an unpinned vsomeip.

# Operator site descriptors: real addresses, account names, and interface names
# that encode a MAC. Never in the image, never in git.
*.local.toml
site.toml

# Keys and anything key-shaped.
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
